1
00:00:00,096 --> 00:00:03,686
Using the UFW firewall is good. It works on

2
00:00:03,862 --> 00:00:06,122
any Linux system in the end. It's therefore

3
00:00:06,122 --> 00:00:07,067
a great choice.

4
00:00:07,724 --> 00:00:10,209
But if you're using a provider that has a

5
00:00:10,209 --> 00:00:12,132
built-in firewall solution, which many

6
00:00:12,132 --> 00:00:12,805
providers do,

7
00:00:13,414 --> 00:00:15,401
you might wanna use that instead for a

8
00:00:15,401 --> 00:00:17,485
couple of reasons. One big reason is that

9
00:00:17,485 --> 00:00:19,745
you can configure it from outside the VPS.

10
00:00:20,161 --> 00:00:22,918
So if you had logged out yourself, you could

11
00:00:22,934 --> 00:00:25,274
change it from outside so you would not

12
00:00:25,274 --> 00:00:27,646
really be locked out. That would be one big

13
00:00:27,678 --> 00:00:29,569
reason for using that firewall instead.

14
00:00:30,162 --> 00:00:32,854
It's probably also a bit easier to quickly

15
00:00:32,886 --> 00:00:35,097
change something here. It has a nice visual

16
00:00:35,129 --> 00:00:35,914
user interface

17
00:00:36,795 --> 00:00:39,182
and it's also not something that runs on

18
00:00:39,278 --> 00:00:41,825
your VPS. It doesn't consume resources

19
00:00:41,857 --> 00:00:42,050
there.

20
00:00:42,578 --> 00:00:45,766
It can't be hacked if your VPS were to be

21
00:00:45,766 --> 00:00:46,856
hacked in some way.

22
00:00:47,689 --> 00:00:50,813
It's simply an extra layer of security to

23
00:00:50,813 --> 00:00:54,626
use that provider-provided firewall. So if

24
00:00:54,674 --> 00:00:56,805
you have it, you may wanna consider using

25
00:00:56,901 --> 00:00:59,176
it. So here on Hetzner, I'll create a new

26
00:00:59,240 --> 00:00:59,769
firewall.

27
00:01:00,442 --> 00:01:00,683
Free,

28
00:01:01,229 --> 00:01:02,995
and as mentioned, other providers have

29
00:01:02,995 --> 00:01:03,862
similar features.

30
00:01:04,521 --> 00:01:05,693
And here I wanna allow

31
00:01:06,367 --> 00:01:09,996
access to port 22, which is the SSH port via

32
00:01:09,996 --> 00:01:12,293
TCP. That is fine, that is the underlying

33
00:01:12,389 --> 00:01:14,187
protocol we're using with SSH.

34
00:01:14,894 --> 00:01:17,287
And I want to allow that access from any IP

35
00:01:17,367 --> 00:01:19,535
address for now. We'll change that later to

36
00:01:19,535 --> 00:01:21,767
make it even more secure, but for now, any

37
00:01:21,783 --> 00:01:24,320
IP, so any computer on the entire world

38
00:01:24,689 --> 00:01:27,515
could connect to our server via port 22.

39
00:01:28,303 --> 00:01:30,864
Which sounds insecure, but keep in mind they

40
00:01:30,864 --> 00:01:33,474
will still need the SSH key, which only we

41
00:01:33,602 --> 00:01:36,196
have on our system. So it's still very

42
00:01:36,196 --> 00:01:36,550
secure.

43
00:01:37,450 --> 00:01:39,051
But again, we'll lock that down even more

44
00:01:39,181 --> 00:01:39,488
later.

45
00:01:40,297 --> 00:01:42,125
You could add more here.

46
00:01:43,902 --> 00:01:45,226
So you could add another rule

47
00:01:46,453 --> 00:01:50,183
and for example, grant access to port 443 if

48
00:01:50,183 --> 00:01:52,412
you had a web server that is able to

49
00:01:53,255 --> 00:01:55,835
resolve TLS, so that is able to handle

50
00:01:55,851 --> 00:01:58,025
secured connections running on that machine.

51
00:01:58,686 --> 00:01:59,456
You could open that.

52
00:02:00,049 --> 00:02:02,886
But here I'll keep it focused on port 22.

53
00:02:03,912 --> 00:02:06,845
You can add outbound rules, but by default,

54
00:02:07,102 --> 00:02:09,779
all outbound traffic is allowed. So if you

55
00:02:09,843 --> 00:02:12,135
run something on your VPS and you're sending

56
00:02:12,167 --> 00:02:15,117
a request to the World Wide Web, like we did

57
00:02:15,117 --> 00:02:17,842
when we ran apt install, for example, we

58
00:02:17,858 --> 00:02:19,413
were installing something

59
00:02:20,022 --> 00:02:22,315
with the package manager where we downloaded

60
00:02:22,347 --> 00:02:24,751
the package from some other website. That

61
00:02:24,751 --> 00:02:27,043
kind of traffic, that outbound traffic is

62
00:02:27,076 --> 00:02:29,304
allowed by default. You could restrict it,

63
00:02:29,320 --> 00:02:31,549
but you don't have to. But it's the inbound

64
00:02:31,549 --> 00:02:33,427
traffic you typically wanna worry about.

65
00:02:34,277 --> 00:02:36,380
So I'll not do anything about the outbound

66
00:02:36,444 --> 00:02:39,204
rules and I'll apply that to that one server

67
00:02:39,573 --> 00:02:41,306
where I set up the firewall.

68
00:02:42,397 --> 00:02:44,098
You could give it a label, could give it a

69
00:02:44,098 --> 00:02:45,575
name, but I'll just create it.

70
00:02:46,152 --> 00:02:48,961
And so now the VPS I created before has this

71
00:02:48,961 --> 00:02:50,870
firewall attached to it.

72
00:02:52,090 --> 00:02:54,529
And now we could theoretically remove or

73
00:02:54,577 --> 00:02:56,872
disable the UFW firewall here

74
00:02:57,546 --> 00:02:59,960
And it would still be protected because we

75
00:02:59,960 --> 00:03:02,486
had the Hetzner firewall in my case. But

76
00:03:02,534 --> 00:03:04,497
here for demo purposes, I'll keep both

77
00:03:04,561 --> 00:03:04,963
running. But

